n-off for  these voters um there are many many  reasons some of them are historical so  if you look at the countries that many  people from ethnic minorities tend to  come from emigrate from you know I speak  as a first generation immigrant myself  they tend to be socialist country so  socialist politics is more normal so we  already have a huge barrier in terms in  terms of them actually showing people  there are the types of politics that  that you can do so that's the first one  the second one is that other parties  labor in particular really labor has  been better historically at going out to  you know doing outreach with these  voters and that's something that the  party is doing a lot of I mean do you  think that some of the issues are the  allegations for example around  Islamophobia in the concept of party I  mean the former chair Barry r/c said  it's very widespread but it's being  effectively ignored I mean is it  something that the concept I party need  to take more seriously I completely  disagree with the barrenness varsity's  view on that we take every single  allegation of Islamophobia seriously and  where we do find party members or people  who hold positions and local government  doing things we tend we suspend them we  investigate why is it the most Muslim  councillor person's called for an  inquiry I don't think the Muslim Council  of Britain is um is an organisation that  would look very favorably on the  conservative party anyway so I think  that there's a probably a political  motive there when I have looked at the  cases that we've talked about I've seen  strong investigations which had been  done fairly and there are many people in  the party who are Muslims who don't  recognize the allegations about sis Lama  phobia okay and now you are in charge of  candidate selection a recent report by  the Fawcett Society found that female  parliamentary candidates are still  facing barriers to entry getting asked  questions for example about what the  h